You're right that DT2GS reuses MCS's actor entirely. The full MCS/DT2GS actor pipeline is:

encode_entity — parse raw obs into entity groups (agents, enemies, etc.)
VAESkillGenerator — entity obs → VAE → latent skill z
input_embedding + skill_embedding — project obs and skills into transformer dim
tblocks — transformer encoder blocks (self-attention over entity embeddings)
toprobs — project to hidden_size
act_layer — output actions
SESiL's actor pipeline is:

Flat obs (no entity parsing)
EncoderPopulation — flat obs → VAE encoder i → skill z (population of M encoders)
policy_head — concat [flat_obs, skill] → MLP (two linear + ReLU)
act_layer — output actions
So the key difference you're asking about is step 3–4: transformer blocks vs MLP for the action/policy part 
(after skill generation, before action output).

To answer your question: Adding transformer blocks to SESiL's policy head (between VAE output and action layer) 
is independent of evolution — evolution only touches the VAE encoders. So yes, you could upgrade the policy head 
to use transformers without affecting the evolutionary merging at all. It would give SESiL stronger representation 
capacity for the action decision, which could matter on SMAC where entity relationships are important.

But for AliceBob (small obs, few agents), the MLP is likely sufficient and transformers would just add parameters 
without much benefit. The question is really about SMAC scalability.

